{"text": " 30 years is long enough that no election survives it. No Fed chair, no news cycle, no administration, which means a 30-year rate isn't a reaction to a headline. It's the closest thing there is to an honest opinion on a country. And sitting in the middle of it is the number everybody's talking about, the 30-year Treasury yield. It's important because it acts as a gauge for the economy. It captures structural economic conditions like long-run inflation expectations, monetary policy decisions, and potential economic growth. And right now, it seems like every country across the world is losing control, and the country sitting in the eye of the storm is the United States. But we need to back up first. On August 18th, the 30-year touched the highest level in 19 years. A few days ago, the United States government went out to borrow $22 billion of 30-year debt. The problem was, at what cost? The sale cleared at a high yield a little over 5.3%, the most expensive government bond sale of its kind since 2001. This comes a few weeks after America became the first country in history to cross $40 trillion in national debt.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.04, "text": " 30 years is long enough that no election survives it.", "tokens": [50363, 1542, 812, 318, 890, 1576, 326, 645, 3071, 36417, 340, 13, 50529], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1161457896232605, "compression_ratio": 1.623456790123457, "no_speech_prob": 0.01146903820335865, "words": [{"word": " 30", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.16, "probability": 0.34015801548957825}, {"word": " years", "start": 0.16, "end": 0.46, "probability": 0.9779101610183716}, {"word": " is", "start": 0.46, "end": 0.7, "probability": 0.9903373718261719}, {"word": " long", "start": 0.7, "end": 0.96, "probability": 0.995750904083252}, {"word": " enough", "start": 0.96, "end": 1.24, "probability": 0.9969127178192139}, {"word": " that", "start": 1.24, "end": 1.56, "probability": 0.9518885016441345}, {"word": " no",